      "id": "4f1c7a92b3d85e04",
      "text": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n<task id=\"inspect-a-topicmap-sign\" xml:lang=\"en\">\n  <title>Inspect a Topicmap Sign</title>\n  <shortdesc>Open the Topicmap of an edit proposal in the local Catalog, press on a Topic sign and on a Role edge, and reach the projected Topic, the TMDM object and the TMDM Role that they stand for.</shortdesc>\n  <taskbody>\n    <prereq>\n      <p>A local review Catalog started with <filepath>scripts/serve-ctm-page-authoring.lisp</filepath> from a commit that has the Role edge gesture or a later one, serving a site root that holds the page <codeph>ctm-and-tmdm-in-hyperdoc</codeph>. A Catalog started from an older commit does not offer the gesture on a Role edge. The concepts are in <xref href=\"hyperdoc:item:4f1c7a92b3d85e01\" scope=\"local\" format=\"dita\">Topicmap Signs and Represented Objects</xref> and <xref href=\"hyperdoc:item:4f1c7a92b3d85e02\" scope=\"local\" format=\"dita\">TMDM Roles and Projected Edges</xref>; the Operations are in <xref href=\"hyperdoc:item:4f1c7a92b3d85e03\" scope=\"local\" format=\"dita\">Topicmap Gesture Operations</xref>.</p>\n    </prereq>\n    <context>\n      <p>Each step names the view or the Operation that it uses. Nothing in this task writes to the wiki: preparing a proposal and looking at its Topicmap are reading.</p>\n    </context>\n    <steps>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Open the Item and prepare a proposal.</cmd>\n        <info>Open the Item E3 (<xref href=\"hyperdoc:function:dreyeck/work/ctm-page-authoring:registered-story-item\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">registered-story-item</xref>) from the Work Breakdown page <codeph>Author the CTM Plan Page</codeph>. In its Edit view choose <b>Prepare edit proposal</b>. The pane to the right shows an <xref href=\"hyperdoc:class:dreyeck/fedwiki-item-edit-preview:item-edit-proposal\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">item-edit-proposal</xref>. A completion shows the same Topicmap with more objects.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Open the Topicmap view of the proposal.</cmd>\n        <info>Choose the tab <b>Topicmap</b>. The native Topicmap draws the objects of the Item edit, each Association as a node of its own and one edge for each Role. The Point panel below the drawing names the Topic at the Point. The Point starts at the proposal.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Press the secondary button on the node <codeph>proposes</codeph> and wait.</cmd>\n        <info>Hold the secondary button on the sign of the Association node for about half a second. The radial menu appears with two entries: <b>Inspect represented object</b> to the left and <b>Inspect Topicmap sign</b> upward. Move in the direction of the entry and release. Releasing without a move chooses nothing and opens nothing. This is the Binding of the radial menu.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Do the same without waiting.</cmd>\n        <info>Press the secondary button, move to the left at once and release. This is the learned mark; it completes with the same Operation and opens the same object as the menu, which you can check by the identical object address in the pane title.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Inspect a Topic sign.</cmd>\n        <info>Upward opens the projected Topic, an item-edit-topic whose Provenance view says how HyperDoc knows the Association and why. To the left opens the TMDM Association it represents. The two are different objects (see the concept topic).</info>\n        <stepresult>The Point and the History are as they were: a secondary press is a reading Operation.</stepresult>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Inspect a Role edge.</cmd>\n        <info>Press the secondary button on the edge between <codeph>proposes</codeph> and <codeph>the-proposed-action</codeph>. The edge has a transparent hit line about 14 pixels wide, below the Topic signs. The menu offers only <b>Inspect represented object</b>; there is no Relation Contract. Choosing it, by the menu or by a mark to the left, opens the TMDM Role.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Navigate from the Role to its objects.</cmd>\n        <info>In the pane of the Role choose the tab <b>Slots</b>. The slots <codeph>parent</codeph>, <codeph>type</codeph> and <codeph>player</codeph> open the TMDM Association, the Role type and the player.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Return to the projection and its provenance.</cmd>\n        <info>In the Topicmap choose a Topic with a primary click: the Point moves there and its panel shows the provenance and the Roles. The Provenance view of a projected Topic ends in a link back to the Topicmap. The History is shown by <b>Open the Workspace as a pane</b>.</info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Compare PRIMARY in the native Topicmap and in TALA.</cmd>\n        <info>In the native Topicmap a primary click on a Topic sign moves the Point and records the History; a primary click on a Role edge does nothing. Choose <b>Lay out with TALA</b> and then the tab <b>TALA (interactive)</b>: there a primary click on a Topic or an edge opens its object in a pane and no Point exists. A secondary press offers the same Operations on the Topic groups, and on a Role edge the single Operation Inspect represented object.</info>\n      </step>\n    </steps>\n    <result>\n      <p>Every object named in the steps is an object of the running image: the pane titles carry its class and address, and two panes of one object show one address.</p>\n    </result>\n    <postreq>\n      <p>What this task cannot show: the automated browser tests dispatch synthetic pointer events (<codeph>isTrusted</codeph> is false). They exercise the element, the Gesture transport, the reducer and the Operations, and prove nothing about a physical mouse or trackpad, for example whether a trackpad delivers a secondary press, a move and a release as one sequence. Try the steps with a mouse first and with the trackpad second; if the mouse works and the trackpad does not, the input device is the cause, not the Operations.</p>\n    </postreq>\n  </taskbody>\n</task>\n",
      "ditaVersion": "1.3",
      "infoType": "task"
    }
  ],
  "journal": [
    {
      "type": "create",
      "id": "4f1c7a92b3d85eff",
      "item": {
        "title": "Topicmap Inspection (DITA)",
        "story": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "id": "4f1c7a92b3d85e00",
            "text": "Topicmap inspection in DITA: the two Operations Inspect Topicmap sign and Inspect represented object, and what they open. Four topics in reading order: Topicmap Signs and Represented Objects (concept), TMDM Roles and Projected Edges (concept), Topicmap Gesture Operations (reference), Inspect a Topicmap Sign (task). Each is a story Item of type dita that holds one DITA 1.3 topic as XML. The Work item that carries them is topicmap-interaction-documentation in Work Breakdown."
          },
          {
            "type": "dita",
            "id": "4f1c7a92b3d85e01",
            "text": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE concept PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Concept//EN\" \"concept.dtd\">\n<concept id=\"topicmap-signs-and-represented-objects\" xml:lang=\"en\">\n  <title>Topicmap Signs and Represented Objects</title>\n  <shortdesc>The sign you press on, the Topic that the Topicmap projection holds for it, the TMDM object that Topic represents and the Lisp objects the projection was derived from are five different things. Inspect Topicmap sign opens the projected Topic; Inspect represented object opens the TMDM object it represents.</shortdesc>\n  <conbody>\n    <section>\n      <title>Five things</title>\n      <p>The Topicmap of an Item edit is drawn from a Topic Map that the topic-maps reader makes of a CTM text. The CTM text is derived from the Lisp objects of the Item edit. Between the Lisp objects and the pixels there are five distinct things, and the two inspection Operations of the marking menu (see <xref href=\"hyperdoc:item:4f1c7a92b3d85e03\" scope=\"local\" format=\"dita\">Topicmap Gesture Operations</xref>) reach two of them.</p>\n      <dl>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Browser SVG sign, a concrete gesture occurrence</dt>\n          <dd>One SVG element drawn for one Topic in one pane. In the native Topicmap it is the transparent rectangle of class <codeph>dreyeck-topicmap-workspace-action</codeph> over the Topic; in <codeph>TALA (interactive)</codeph> it is the group <codeph>g</codeph> of the Topic. Gesture input is bound to this element. An occurrence (<xref href=\"hyperdoc:class:dreyeck/inspector/topicmap:workspace-action-sign-occurrence\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">workspace-action-sign-occurrence</xref>) records the Topic, the projection, the Workspace if there is one, the pane and the element. A refresh replaces the element and the occurrence ends with it; the same Topic drawn again is a new occurrence.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>HyperDoc Topicmap projection</dt>\n          <dd>A <xref href=\"hyperdoc:class:dreyeck/topicmap:topicmap-projection\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">topicmap-projection</xref>: the Topics and Associations that the renderers draw, with their positions and visibility. It is derived from the Lisp objects each time a view is shown; <xref href=\"hyperdoc:function:dreyeck/item-edit-ctm:current-projection\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">current-projection</xref> keeps the last one of an origin object while its CTM text is unchanged, so that the Workspace Point survives a refresh.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Projected Topic</dt>\n          <dd>An <xref href=\"hyperdoc:class:dreyeck/item-edit-ctm:item-edit-topic\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">item-edit-topic</xref>. It has an id (its key in the Topic Map), a label, view properties, the TMDM object it represents, and its origin, the CTM of the Item edit. It also carries the provenance: it is an object of the Item edit, an Association of the Item edit, or a schema Topic.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Represented TMDM object</dt>\n          <dd>What <xref href=\"hyperdoc:function:dreyeck/topicmap:topicmap-topic-object-of\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">topicmap-topic-object-of</xref> returns for the projected Topic: a TMDM Topic (<xref href=\"hyperdoc:class:topic-maps.tmdm:topic\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">topic</xref>) or a TMDM Association (<xref href=\"hyperdoc:class:topic-maps.tmdm:association\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">association</xref>) of the Topic Map that the topic-maps reader made.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Original Lisp objects</dt>\n          <dd>The objects the CTM text is derived from: the edit proposal, the preview or the completion, the Story Item as observed, the Action. <xref href=\"hyperdoc:function:dreyeck/item-edit-ctm:project-item-edit\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">project-item-edit</xref> reads them and changes nothing. The projection keeps for every Topic which Lisp object it came from and how that is known, observed or derived. That provenance is part of the HyperDoc projection and is not stated in the Topic Map.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n      </dl>\n    </section>\n    <section>\n      <title>Example: the Association node proposes</title>\n      <p>The example is the edit proposal for the Item E3 (<codeph>ed37e9bac2fcd548</codeph>) of the page <codeph>ctm-and-tmdm-in-hyperdoc</codeph>. The Topicmap of a completion draws the same node among more objects. The references below open the objects of the proposal that the Catalog prepares from that page file; preparing it writes nothing, and a Catalog that does not serve that page answers with an explanation instead.</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>The Association <codeph>proposes</codeph> is drawn as a node of its own, with the id <codeph>proposes(action=the-proposed-action,proposal=the-proposal)</codeph>. Two edges join it to its players, one for each Role. The proposal itself is <xref href=\"hyperdoc:example:proposal\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">the proposal</xref> and the Topicmap is made from <xref href=\"hyperdoc:example:ctm\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">its CTM</xref>.</li>\n        <li><b>Inspect Topicmap sign</b> on the node opens the projected Topic <xref href=\"hyperdoc:example:proposes-topic\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">proposes</xref>, an <codeph>item-edit-topic</codeph>. Its Provenance view says that it is an Association of the Item edit, known as derived, because the function <codeph>proposal-action</codeph> makes the Action from the proposal now and the proposal holds none.</li>\n        <li><b>Inspect represented object</b> on the same node opens the TMDM Association <xref href=\"hyperdoc:example:proposes-association\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">proposes</xref> that the projected Topic represents: a TMDM Association of type <codeph>proposes</codeph> with the Scope <codeph>derived</codeph> and two Roles.</li>\n        <li>From the projected Topic, the Provenance table links the Lisp objects: <xref href=\"hyperdoc:example:proposal\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">the proposal</xref> as player of the Role <codeph>proposal</codeph> and <xref href=\"hyperdoc:example:the-proposed-action-object\" scope=\"external\" format=\"hyperdoc\">the proposed Action</xref> as player of the Role <codeph>action</codeph>.</li>\n      </ol>\n    </section>\n    <section>\n      <title>Why the projected Topic and the TMDM object are not the same</title>\n      <ul>\n        <li>They are objects of different classes with different content. The projected Topic holds the provenance, the position and the visibility; the TMDM Association holds its type, its Scope and its Roles and nothing else. The reason why HyperDoc states the Association as derived is kept in the projection; the Topic Map carries only the class as the Scope.</li>\n        <li>They have different lifetimes. The projection is made again when a view is shown, and the Topic Map is read from the CTM text; an equal CTM text gives a new Topic Map object.</li>\n        <li>They are joined by one link: the projected Topic holds the TMDM object and reports it as <codeph>topicmap-topic-object-of</codeph>. Inspect Topicmap sign ends at the projected Topic; Inspect represented object follows that link and ends at the other side of it.</li>\n        <li>The key of the projected Topic is not an identifier of the TMDM object. For an Association node it is made from the type and the Roles, <codeph>type(role=player,...)</codeph>, so that two reads of one CTM text give the same key.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </section>\n  </conbody>\n</concept>\n",
            "ditaVersion": "1.3",
            "infoType": "concept"
          },
